{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/session-18868900-demo-budget.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous demos: transfer and stopping audit

This session supplied four sharply different examples: a 28×14 input yielding a singleton color, two large periodic/lattice inputs yielding compact 4×6 and 5×3 grids, and a 22×27 lattice yielding 5×3. Their output shapes and palette encodings differ, while the 27×27 test is a periodic 3/4/9/2/7/1 lattice. The singleton output was not a valid basis for the test shape, and a guessed 6×4 block expansion was rejected. These examples demonstrate that output shape must be inferred independently from palette/marker structure and must replay across relevant demos before submission.

Reusable audit: (1) record input and output dimensions and marker coordinates for every pair; (2) distinguish output-shape rule from color/phase rule; (3) normalize geometry and role colors, then require a single candidate to reproduce complete demos; (4) after rejection, reconsider shape and mapping together rather than making a cosmetic variant; (5) request another demo only if an explicit ambiguity remains and the next pair could distinguish candidate outputs. This session did not establish a validated transformation, so do not treat any proposed test output as a known answer.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/session-18868900-demo-budget.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-role-reconciliation.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous-demo role reconciliation

When demonstrations differ sharply in dimensions, palettes, and object structures, log the exact changed-cell mask and the roles of each color/object separately for every pair. A shared color numeral or visual resemblance is not evidence of a shared transformation. Replay each candidate rule against every complete demo, including untouched cells and identity examples; reject rules that fail any pair. Transfer only a role-level invariant supported across examples. If none is supported, request another demo only when you can name competing hypotheses and the concrete observation that would discriminate them. Avoid speculative large edits based on one salient pattern.

In the reviewed instance, one example changed a single vertical stripe to color 8 while another changed one background-colored column adjacent to a marker into 8, leaving the marker/object untouched. These observations did not establish a common rule for the test grid, which had a broad color-6 pattern and color-2 marker stripes. The useful lesson is to reconcile changed masks and roles before transferring, not to infer a task rule from palette numerals or one visual resemblance.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/heterogeneous-demo-role-reconciliation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/session-18868900-output-shape-review.md", "content": "# Session review: periodic lattice to compact output

This session is a cautionary example of under-validated transfer. Demos supplied repetitive stripe/lattice grids with compact outputs of several different dimensions (5×6, 2×4, 5×4, 3×2), while the test had a 28×27 periodic lattice. A small guessed grid based on salient marker cells was submitted without a demonstrated derivation of its dimensions or cell mapping; it was incorrect. A second answer likewise used an unvalidated extraction rule and was incorrect.

Reusable lesson: before transferring a periodic or lattice transformation, explicitly derive both (a) output dimensions and (b) the mapping from complete input units/coordinates to output cells, using every demo. Do not confuse a visually plausible marker subset with the actual extraction rule. After binary rejection, it is not enough to alter one edge or color: reassess shape and mapping together, and request a demo only when a specific competing mapping can be distinguished.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/session-18868900-output-shape-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-turn-selection.md", "content": "# Selecting demos for heterogeneous ARC examples

When available demonstrations differ sharply in grid size, palette, and object structure, log each pair's role colors, exact changed-cell mask, untouched/identity regions, and any plausible structural trigger. Treat a rule as supported only if it reproduces complete outputs, not merely the salient changed motif.

Before requesting another pair, name the competing hypotheses and the concrete observation that would distinguish them; ask only if that distinction can materially change the test prediction. Repeated requests without a targeted discriminator have cost but may not improve inference. In the same way, do not progress from one vivid example directly to a large speculative output: preserve the test input unless evidence supports a transformation. Keep this evidence-gathering discipline compatible with strict interfaces: each puzzle response still emits exactly one action JSON.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/heterogeneous-demo-turn-selection.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/session-demo-request-ledger.md", "content": "# Demo requests need an evidence ledger

In a multi-demo ARC instance, receiving a demonstration should trigger analysis before another request: record the dimensions and palette, distinguish unchanged cells from changed cells, and summarize what each pair says about candidate rules. Maintain the specific unresolved question that another demo could answer. Request again only if a plausible response could discriminate live hypotheses; after collecting heterogeneous examples, test one role-based rule against every pair rather than trusting surface resemblance to the test. This preserves the demo budget and prevents a sequence of requests from replacing inference.

In strict one-action turns, submit only the next action JSON. Do not append commentary or duplicate action objects, which can make the response invalid or obscure the selected action.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/session-demo-request-ledger.md"}
